Project Overview

This project demonstrates how to securely host private content from Amazon S3 on an Amazon EC2 web server without using hardcoded credentials. 
It focuses on the Security Pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework by implementing IAM Roles for service-to-service communication.

Architecture

- S3 Bucket: Stores a private image file (no public access).
- IAM Role: Granted S3ReadOnlyAccess permissions.
- EC2 Instance: Launched with the IAM Role attached (Instance Profile).
- User Data: A bootstrap script that installs Apache and fetches the image via the AWS CLI.

Implementation Steps

1. Security & Identity (IAM)

- Created an IAM Role for EC2.
- Attached the managed policy AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess.

This allows the EC2 instance to fetch objects from S3 using temporary security tokens instead of permanent Access Keys.

2. Storage (S3)

- Created a private S3 bucket.
- Uploaded my-image.png.
- Verified that the object is not accessible via a public URL.

3. Compute (EC2)

- Launched a t2.micro instance using Amazon Linux 2023.
- Configured Security Groups to allow HTTP (Port 80) traffic.
- Applied the following User Data script to automate the setup:

User Data Script

To automate the server setup, I used a Bash script. [View the full installation script here](https://github.com/Y-Hus-Git/aws-saa-projects/blob/4fb019f21b5acf51a2b6f558b58052a32f7a151b/scripts/user_data.sh)



Validation

- Accessed the EC2 Public IPv4 address in a web browser.
- Confirmed the image displays correctly, proving the EC2 instance successfully authenticated with S3 using its assigned Role.

Cleanup

- Terminated the EC2 instance.
- Deleted the S3 bucket and IAM Role to avoid unnecessary costs.
